#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139522} :activity/id #uuid "4e2e5333-ed8e-576e-abed-94cc93efbdb5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049051 :topic/id #uuid "d5e55293-c14f-55db-a945-4aa2b42df552" :topic/math-academy-id 2310} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139522 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139662 :tutorial/content "A **compound inequality** is an inequality that consists of multiple different inequalities put together. For example, the compound inequality \n\n$$\nx \\leq 0 \\hspace{.25cm} \\text{ or } \\hspace{.25cm} x \\gt  3\n$$\n\n states that $x$ is less than or equal to $0$, or greater than $3$.\n\nFor the compound \"or\" inequality to be satisfied, at least one of the separate inequalities must be satisfied. To illustrate:\n\n- $x=-1$ satisfies the inequality because it satisfies $x \\leq 0.$\n\n- $x=5$ satisfies the inequality because it satisfies $x > 3.$\n\n- $x=1$ does not satisfy the inequality because it does not satisfy $x \\leq 0$ and it also does not satisfy $x > 3.$\n\nTo graph a compound inequality involving the word \"or,\" we graph the two separate inequalities on the same number line. In this particular case,\n\n- the inequality $x \\leq 0$ consists of a closed circle at $0$ and an arrow to the left, while\n\n- the inequality $x >3$ consists of an open circle at $3$ and an arrow to the right.\n\nThe resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/96/96f1b1ee25ebc210a83ba12d90396683b7e2660050f820c4057ab85de419db91.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "770f70a7-6710-533c-bd9a-e37123e913bb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8132 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "73ffc612-4a54-5b2a-9949-3131ab4b5010" :step/math-academy-id 79780 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139523}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138536 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138073 :question/id #uuid "014723fc-0b7d-5212-a41e-73847f5adb00" :question/math-academy-id "e-13011"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6090342d-3d4e-519f-ab7d-3fb6df6e92a8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050519}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481665}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing \"Or\" Inequalities on Number Lines"} :step/id #uuid "1d905dee-c60b-587c-8b1e-96a234f4883a" :step/math-academy-id 79781 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139524}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139524 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138537 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138075 :question/id #uuid "e5b6a9dc-3a19-5496-9e22-d953ff4eb647" :question/math-academy-id "e-13018"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32daa2fb-e5fa-5e44-b6f2-5d815d9145af" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491986}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying \"Or\" Inequalities Given on Number Lines"} :step/id #uuid "bcf79e23-3398-56f2-9f7c-6dddc11d8b32" :step/math-academy-id 79784 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139525}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139525 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139663 :tutorial/content "Compound inequalities can also be written with the word \"and.\" However, such inequalities are often written together as a single expression with two inequality signs. For example, the compound inequality \n\n$$\n-4 \\leq x < -1\n$$\n\n means that $-4 \\leq x$ and $x < -1.$\n\nFor the compound \"and\" inequality to be satisfied, both of the separate inequalities must be satisfied. To illustrate:\n\n- $x=-3$ satisfies the inequality because it satisfies both $-4 \\leq x$ and $x < -1.$\n\n- $x=-5$ does not satisfy the inequality because it does not satisfy $-4 \\leq x.$\n\n- $x=0$ does not satisfy the inequality because it does not satisfy $x < -1.$\n\nTo graph this compound inequality, we first graph the two inequalities separately.\n\n![](images/42/42edf8ee61badbbd1e5525a3843cfbe189e6432b2ad06455c1bdca6a7087ac56.png)\n\nThen, we keep only where they overlap.\n\n![](images/fa/fa07233f96bed30ddfcc5905a55a835763359cd34188e4d1c249c13b1d27d25c.png)\n\n**Note:** Any compound \"and\" inequality will always represent a segment between two points. So, it is not always necessary to draw both inequalities and find the overlap. To speed up the process, we can just draw the endpoints with open or closed circles as appropriate, and highlight the area between the endpoints." :tutorial/id #uuid "9859e4f2-2267-558c-923f-35c348957ad9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8133 :tutorial/title "Compound \"And\" Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "59dbf0dd-b3a9-5867-8cbf-3b28624f9ad9" :step/math-academy-id 79788 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139526}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139526 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138538 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138074 :question/id #uuid "21eace75-0b5a-5b37-903c-c238e3821bee" :question/math-academy-id "e-13012"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9106723c-b8b0-586e-8c2b-9bb4f50bc945" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492061}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing \"And\" Inequalities on Number Lines"} :step/id #uuid "7e38370d-1c18-5d27-bb22-cf9444dd1b90" :step/math-academy-id 79789 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139527}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139527 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138539 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138076 :question/id #uuid "347520ff-e759-5d99-acf7-0bc8a8f26689" :question/math-academy-id "e-13019"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a707a741-0208-5a74-8478-ac05ff2673ab" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492032}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying \"And\" Inequalities Given on Number Lines"} :step/id #uuid "40b2748c-2bc7-5064-b788-ba5145c312f9" :step/math-academy-id 79793}] :activity/title "Compound Inequalities" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138614}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492094} :activity/id #uuid "9814bdeb-c5c1-549b-8942-0dcd8592f0fd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049051 :topic/id #uuid "d5e55293-c14f-55db-a945-4aa2b42df552" :topic/math-academy-id 2310} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03910583-d566-5a8b-bfc2-8972fe3ecdd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f6/f6139cea308db19681b8e2239e01f77d4af406a728041aa738b29ee3ce6a603e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491454} {:answer/id #uuid "7b650c21-47ed-5214-a59e-48d2075d1b3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/90/901ffc463b1516e30690f41af6225c62a1a3cc3950bc34e28bc5b13ae7314b28.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491672} {:answer/id #uuid "8698ddac-ea80-50d9-9ad8-a8742ba97aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/24/2415d02b1c88fbeb254f41d3540b81e20be8fb94da400b77fabca65e40da3b8c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491685} {:answer/id #uuid "97ae2684-9fda-5d9f-bda1-046e9cd84f79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6f/6fa6ef603a454d9f219ca5967a87a99cc8afabc30105d36879c172b8385fd36b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491718} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0ec700-72a5-535a-a4a1-e48679183f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/49/496986cb7dca8bffb2d2856f3867e48d794f106139d4703e1b2166d9a223781d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491756}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491454} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9c1885b-25c3-55b9-8caf-90ff3bd587c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d2934ac4-af1a-5b63-bae8-4d35ad544ba0" :question/math-academy-id "q-54810" :question/problem "Which of the following number lines represents the inequality $-3<x<1?$" :question/worked-solution "The inequality $-3<x<1$ means that $x$ can take any value greater than $-3,$ and less than $1.$\n\nTo represent this on a number line, we draw an open circle at $-3$ and another open circle at $1.$ Then, we highlight the region between the points.\n\n![](images/f6/f6139cea308db19681b8e2239e01f77d4af406a728041aa738b29ee3ce6a603e.png)"} :step/id #uuid "1a122adf-7490-5a96-86fc-64fdc36ac5fa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492092}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492092 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0158d2c2-46e2-511e-9829-663aca526316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x\\le -1$ or $x\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491450} {:answer/id #uuid "2fd0b12d-c6d3-5074-bd89-cbaa31e2e2ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x<-1$ or $x>1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491548} {:answer/id #uuid "3f38c2dd-7e7e-5407-9e8b-c247c54b9afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x>-1$ or $x>1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491579} {:answer/id #uuid "7853872b-6305-5952-a1e3-1e4da4e83d64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x<-1$ or $x<1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491663} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ce2a8a-1854-5692-8ab6-ce6a4ef7ea6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x\\ge -1$ or $x\\le 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491747}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491548} :answer-field/id #uuid "81599ce3-b5fd-5790-a91f-aa92a6b6fa9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "087d9e13-8fc3-51e2-a62c-c006b17d535f" :question/math-academy-id "q-54820" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f2fc2248ce56cd8209d9f6438d24c7f9e9f805f320ef5549be25282cfbaa6e61.png)\n\nWhich inequality corresponds to the highlighted region shown on the number line above?" :question/worked-solution "The red rays contain all values less than  $-1$ or greater than $1.$ In addition,\n\n- the open circle at $-1$ implies that $x$ cannot be equal to $-1,$ and\n\n- the open circle at $1$ implies that $x$ cannot be equal to $1.$\n\nTherefore, the highlighted region represents $x<-1$ or $x>1.$"} :step/id #uuid "471f4a51-0ae6-5b3b-b0f1-91ebb382a932"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492094 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492032 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05fd7dc9-4b77-586d-9ed5-f96227aed75b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2<x<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491463} {:answer/id #uuid "1b458bce-037d-59e4-a297-afaa3a6e5449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2<x\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491508} {:answer/id #uuid "5101b5ff-6f7e-5771-aeaf-1f3ea7c2baa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491607} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb5ca05-d4de-59e6-8172-825f719298fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x\\le 2$ or $x\\ge -2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491722} {:answer/id #uuid "f6121d20-e884-5a07-9ef4-25a31648abf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\le x<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491875}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491607} :answer-field/id #uuid "9deac3f0-5baf-51d6-9fda-87f6599c1dbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8a1c7bea-76d1-5380-abbc-23d155edc522" :question/math-academy-id "q-28797" :question/problem "![](images/67/67c1fa5db66f96b6bbbccacf251f3cdc655fc88a6b87499234f4b1cd0a21da1d.png)\n\nWhich inequality corresponds to the highlighted region shown on the number line above?" :question/worked-solution "The red segment contains all values between  $-2$ and $2.$\n\n- the closed circle at $-2$ implies that $x$ can be equal to $-2,$ while\n\n- the closed circle at $2$ implies that $x$ can be equal to $2.$\n\nTherefore, the highlighted region represents $-2\\le x\\le 2.$"} :step/id #uuid "5ec340ac-62fa-5a0a-99de-ca8554313a1d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492079}}] :activity/title "Compound Inequalities" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491445}]}
